{"text": " instantaneous velocity and speed. instantaneous velocity. The quantity that tells us how fast an object is moving anywhere along its path is the instantaneous velocity or simply velocity. It is the average velocity between two discrete points on the path. Suppose we know the position x of an object at any time t, then we would know the continuous function x of t. We can express the average velocity by average velocity equals x of t sub 2 minus x of t sub 1 divided by t sub 2 minus t sub 1 equals delta x over delta t. To find the instantaneous velocity at any position, we let t sub 1 approach t and t sub 2 approach t plus delta t. Then we substitute into our expression for the average velocity and define v of t by v of t equals the limit as delta t approaches 0 of x of t plus delta t minus x of t all divided by delta t. This equals dx of t over dt. We now define the instantaneous velocity of an object is the limit of the average velocity as the elapsed time delta t approaches 0 or the derivative of x with respect to t. v of t equals dx of t over dt. Instantaneous velocity is a vector with dimension of length over time. Since the definition of average velocity is written in terms of the change in position delta x over a specific time interval delta t, we also refer to the velocity as the rate of change of the position function. The rate of change is a measure of how fast a function is changing at a time t or the slope of the line that is tangent to the curve at t. The displacement at the initial time t0 is 0, i.e. delta x equals 0. Therefore, the velocity will also be 0 and the slope will be flat. Checkpoint. Pause and check your understanding. On a position versus time graph, what does a horizontal tangent line mean about the velocity at that instant? At other times t1, t2, and so on through t sub-eddin, the velocity is not 0, unless the position never changes because the slope would be positive or negative. If the function x of t had a minimum, then the slope would also be 0, i.e. v of t equals 0. Thus, the zeros of the velocity function give the minimum and maximum of the position function. Account Academy video is linked on the course webpage. Example problem. Plotting velocity from position. The problem. A position versus time graph is shown on the course webpage. Using this graph, determine the corresponding velocity versus time graph. Specifically, identify the velocity during each time interval. Sketch or describe the resulting velocity time graph. The model. The motion is one-dimensional along a straight line. The graph is made of straight line position segments, so the velocity is constant within each time interval. A positive slope represents motion in the positive direction. A zero slope represents zero velocity, and a negative slope represents motion in the negative direction. The math. Part A. The velocity during each interval is the slope of the position versus time graph. During the first interval, the position changes from 0.0 meters to 0.5 meters, while the time changes from 0.0 seconds to 0.5 seconds, which gives v equals 0.5 meters minus 0.0 meters divided by 0.5 seconds minus 0.0 seconds equals 1.0 meters per second. During the second interval, the position remains fixed at 0.5 meters, while the time changes from 0.5 seconds to 1.0 seconds, which gives v equals 0.5 meters minus 0.5 meters divided by 1.0 seconds minus 0.5 seconds equals 0.0 meters per second. During the third interval, the position changes from 0.5 meters to 0.0 meters, while the time changes from 1.0 seconds to 2.0 seconds, which gives v equals 0.0 meters minus 0.5 meters divided by 2.0 seconds minus 1.0 seconds equals negative 0.5 meters per second. Part B. The velocity time graph is therefore piecewise constant with horizontal segments at positive 1.0 meters per second, 0.0 meters per second, and negative 0.5 meters per second over the three corresponding intervals. The conclusion. The velocity is positive 1.0 meters per second from 0 to 0.5 seconds, 0.0 meters per second from 0.5 seconds to 1.0 seconds, and negative 0.5 meters per second from 1.0 seconds to 2.0 seconds. The signs match the physical interpretation of the graph. The object first moves in the positive direction, then stops, and finally moves back toward the origin. The position of a particle moving along the x-axis is given by x of t equals 3.0 meters per second times t, plus 0.5 meters per second cubed times t cubed. Part A. Using the definition of instantaneous velocity, find the instantaneous velocity at t equals 2.0 seconds. Part B. Calculate the average velocity between t sub 1 equals 1.0 seconds, and t sub 2 equals 3.0 seconds. The model. The particle moves along the x-axis, so its motion is one-dimensional. Instantaneous velocity describes the rate at which the position changes at a single instant, while average velocity describes the net change in position over a finite time interval. Because the position function is a polynomial in time, the instantaneous velocity can be found by differentiating the position function directly. The math. Part A. The instantaneous velocity is the derivative of the position function with respect to time, which gives v of t equals dx over dt, which equals the derivative with respect to time of 3.0 meters per second times t, plus 0.5 meters per second cubed times t cubed, equals 3.0 meters per second, plus 1.5 meters per second cubed, t squared. The instantaneous velocity at t equals 2.0 seconds is found by evaluating this function at that time, which gives v of 2.0 seconds equals 3.0 meters per second, plus 1.5 meters per second cubed times 2.0 seconds squared, which equals 9.0 meters per second. Part B. The average velocity between t sub 1 equals 1.0 seconds, and t sub 2 equals 3.0 seconds requires the position at each end point, which gives x of 1.0 seconds equals 3.0 meters per second times 1.0 seconds, plus 0.5 meters per second cubed times 1.0 seconds cubed, equals 3.5 meters. x of 3.0 seconds equals 3.0 meters per second times 3.0 seconds, plus 0.5 meters per second cubed times 3.0 seconds cubed equals 22.5 meters. The average velocity is the displacement over the elapsed time, which gives average velocity equals x of 3.0 seconds minus x of 1.0 seconds divided by 3.0 seconds minus 1.0 seconds. Equals 22.5 meters minus 3.5 meters divided by 2.0 seconds equals 9.5 meters per second. The conclusion. The instantaneous velocity at t equals 2.0 seconds is 9.0 meters per second, and the average velocity from 1.0 seconds to 3.0 seconds is 9.5 meters per second. These values are close but not identical because the velocity is changing over the interval.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.54, "text": " instantaneous velocity and speed.", "tokens": [50364, 45596, 9269, 293, 3073, 13, 50541], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.25260413674747245, "compression_ratio": 1.806930693069307, "no_speech_prob": 0.04432015120983124}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 3.54, "end": 6.24, "text": " instantaneous velocity.", "tokens": [50541, 45596, 9269, 13, 50676], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.25260413674747245, "compression_ratio": 1.806930693069307, "no_speech_prob": 0.04432015120983124}, {"id": 2, "seek": 0, "start": 6.24, "end": 11.68, "text": " The quantity that tells us how fast an object is moving anywhere along its path", "tokens": [50676, 440, 11275, 300, 5112, 505, 577, 2370, 364, 2657, 307, 2684, 4992, 2051, 1080, 3100, 50948], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.25260413674747245, "compression_ratio": 1.806930693069307, "no_speech_prob": 0.04432015120983124}, {"id": 3, "seek": 0, "start": 11.68, "end": 16.14, "text": " is the instantaneous velocity or simply velocity.", "tokens": [50948, 307, 264, 45596, 9269, 420, 2935, 9269, 13, 51171], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.25260413674747245, "compression_ratio": 1.806930693069307, "no_speech_prob": 0.04432015120983124}, {"id": 4, "seek": 0, "start": 16.14, "end": 21.